<Base
  title="About"
  description="Michael Melton is an IT professional in Chino, California, who builds internal tools and platforms for M&A discovery."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <h1 class="page-title">About</h1>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      I'm Michael, an IT professional working remotely in beautiful Chino, California. I've spent
      most of my life around computers. I've always been curious about what makes them tick and how
      to get the most out of them, and over time that curiosity pushed me from running systems to
      building things on top of them.
    </p>
    <p>
      I started my IT career in 2017, and since then I've worked in both small business and
      enterprise environments. Along the way, I put a lot of time into self-directed learning,
      working through online courses and hands-on projects in both IT and software development. I
      also have an associate's degree in business administration from Chaffey College. Because of
      that mix, I've gotten comfortable learning whatever a new problem calls for. If a project
      needs a tool or language I haven't used before, I learn it and get to work.
    </p>
    <p>
      Earlier in my career, I took the lead on a number of projects, including a company-wide
      rollout of Duo (multi-factor authentication), developing security best practices for Linux
      systems, setting up imaging for both Windows and macOS, maintaining the equipment in three
      computer labs, migrating department documentation to SharePoint, and evaluating mobile device
      management solutions. I also led a team of 5 IT analysts and spent a good amount of time
      mentoring colleagues in other roles.
    </p>
    <p>
      Today, I'm a Lead IT Systems Analyst working on mergers and acquisitions, where I lead a team
      of 3 systems analysts. Once a deal closes, we handle the discovery work, figuring out what's
      actually in the acquired company's systems so the integration can be planned around what's
      really there. Early on, that meant pulling reports and checking systems by hand on every deal.
      Eventually, I built internal tooling to automate the discovery process. It now handles the
      repetitive data gathering on its own, so the process scales as the number of acquisitions
      grows, and our time goes to the analysis and decisions that actually need a person. My{" "}
      <a href="/projects/ma-recon-tool/">M&amp;A discovery platform</a> write-up covers it in more
      detail.
    </p>
    <p>
      I enjoy this kind of work because it uses both sides of my background. Knowing how IT
      environments are actually run tells me which details matter, and writing code lets me capture
      them the same way every time. Most of that code is Python and PostgreSQL, though I'm
      comfortable across the stack, from REST APIs to the front end to the deployment pipeline
      behind it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Lately, I've been focused on building systems where AI is a core part of how they work. I'll
      be writing about those projects here as they come together.
    </p>
    <p>
      Outside of work, you'll find me spending time with my wife, son, and two cats, listening to
      music, podcasts, and audiobooks, playing video and board games, working out, watching MMA and
      sci-fi movies, or playing ukulele.
    </p>
    <p>
      The best way to reach me is <a href="mailto:mmelton13@gmail.com">email</a>. You can also find
      me on <a href="https://github.com/mmelton1">GitHub</a> and{" "}
      <a href="https://www.linkedin.com/in/mjmelton">LinkedIn</a>.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
